HUMANIZED_PARAS[37] = 'Solar energy is vital for Ghana\'s renewable electricity targets. Yet, solar panels operating in the field face tough operating conditions. High temperatures, Harmattan dust, and physical stresses damage silicon wafers over time. Common faults such as micro-cracks, surface dirt, and hotspots cut electrical output significantly. When left unattended, these defects cause localized burnouts and fire hazards, costing solar operators substantial revenue every year (IRENA, 2024). To solve this problem, we developed SOLAR SCAN, an edge-based diagnostic inspection system built for offline field work. We fine-tuned a lightweight YOLOv8 object detection model (Jocher et al., 2023) on real-world panel fault datasets and converted it to an INT8 quantized TensorFlow Lite (TFLite) format (TensorFlow Lite, 2024). To prevent false alarms from stray photos, we implemented a two-layer validation gatekeeper: a background COCO filter removes everyday objects, while colour-space histograms filter out vegetation and plain backgrounds. Once cleared, a wafer thermodynamics engine uses Nominal Operating Cell Temperature (NOCT) equations (Duffie & Beckman, 2013) to calculate actual operating temperatures and wattage losses from field irradiance readings. The final 3.2 MB quantized model achieves 92.7% mean Average Precision (mAP@50) with sub-150ms inference latency on standard mobile processors. Field usability testing with 12 technicians and engineering students produced a System Usability Scale (SUS) score of 84.3 out of 100 (Brooke, 1996), proving strong practical utility for solar plant maintenance.'
HUMANIZED_PARAS[38] = 'Keywords: photovoltaic systems, YOLOv8, TensorFlow Lite, edge computing, INT8 quantization, double-layer validation, wafer thermodynamics, NOCT modelling, system usability scale'

